Shells in cyber security are widely used by attackers to remotely control systems, making them an important part of the attack chain. 
In this room, I explored different shells used in offensive security, the differences between them, and their use cases. This 
knowledge can help enhance penetration testing and exploitation skills and also help us understand how to detect when a remote shell 
is being used by an attacker within an organization.

A shell is software that allows a user to interact with an OS. It can be a graphical interface, but it is usually a command-line 
interface, and this will depend on the operating system running on the target system.

REVERSE SHELL
Is the most common shell in pen. testing;
The Attacker prepares a listener, a vulnmachine connects back to the attacker and the atttacker gains a shell.
What makes the Reverse shell popular?
- Many firewalls block incoming connections.
- Outgoing firewalls are often allowed.
- The Tarhet"calls" the attacker instead of waiting for a call.

BIND SHELL
This works the opposite way. The target opens a port and waits. The Attacker then connects directly to that port.
This can be blocked more easily becausemthe target exposes a listening service.

INTERACTIVE vs NON-INTERACTIVE SHELLS
Sometimes, a shell might feel broken when expected commands don't work on it. This is generally termed Non-Interactive shell.
The goal is to often to upgrade it into an interactive TTY shell, which behaves more like a normal shell.

Learning shells allows the attacker or tester to;
- Navigate the File Systems
- Read files
- Check users and permissions
- Continue investigating the system, if you're authorized to do so.


